  ╭─ Methodology ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── How the measurement works ╮  
  │──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│  
  │ • zsh is started the way a terminal starts it -- `zsh -i -c exit`, each start bounded by 10 s -- so the number describes the start you wait through when │  
  │   you open a terminal. The plan runs 5 such starts, and the median is the middle one: not an average, and not a single run.                              │  
  │ • zsh/zprof profiled one further start and reported 6 functions, heaviest by the time each spent on itself, in zprof's own order: 1. compdump -- 725.25  │  
  │   ms total, 725.25 ms self, 1 call(s), 42.01% of the profiled start. 2. compdef -- 306.38 ms total, 306.38 ms self, 1027 call(s), 17.75% of the profiled │  
  │ … and 9 more                                                                                                                                             │  
